WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.159
Dangling access is not limited to manual allocation and manual deletion.

2
00:00:04.159 --> 00:00:11.344
Containers can invalidate pointers, references, and iterators when they grow, shrink, erase elements, clear contents, or otherwise rearrange storage.

3
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:17.016
A pointer to an element may become invalid even while the container itself remains alive.

4
00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:19.663
std::vector reallocation is a common example.

5
00:00:19.663 --> 00:00:24.578
When a vector needs more capacity, it may move elements into new storage.

6
00:00:24.578 --> 00:00:31.006
Pointers, references, and iterators that pointed into the old storage no longer refer to the current elements.

7
00:00:31.006 --> 00:00:35.544
Code that stores those observers across a mutation must be reviewed carefully.

8
00:00:35.544 --> 00:00:39.703
Erase and clear operations also matter because they end element lifetimes.

9
00:00:39.703 --> 00:00:44.619
An iterator or reference to an erased element should not be used later.

10
00:00:44.619 --> 00:00:52.560
Mutation while iterating deserves a deliberate pattern that updates iterators according to the container's rules and avoids keeping stale observers.

11
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.828
Different containers have different invalidation rules.

12
00:00:54.828 --> 00:01:01.257
Some provide more stable node addresses for certain operations, while others prioritize contiguous storage or compact layout.

13
00:01:01.257 --> 00:01:06.550
The choice of container should reflect both performance needs and lifetime requirements for observers.

14
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.466
When long-lived observers are required, stable ownership may be a better design.

15
00:01:11.466 --> 00:01:18.272
That could mean owning objects separately and storing identifiers, handles, or smart pointers according to the lifecycle policy.

16
00:01:18.272 --> 00:01:24.701
The important point is to separate stable identity from temporary container layout when observers outlive ordinary mutations.

17
00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.969
Tests should cover mutation during observation.

18
00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:34.154
Push, reserve, erase, clear, resize, sort, and replacement operations can all reveal whether stored access paths are still valid.

19
00:01:34.154 --> 00:01:40.960
Reviewers should pay special attention to code that caches element addresses or iterators and later mutates the container.
